(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 21-year-old woman in diabetic ketoacidosis has Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 20 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does her breathing pattern accomplish?","choices":["It lowers carbon dioxide to offset the acidosis","It signals impending respiratory failure","It retains carbon dioxide to raise her pH","It is a primary respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis is compensated by increased ventilation to decrease carbon dioxide; in severe cases hyperventilation is profound, as Kussmaul respirations (increased tidal volume and tachypnea) in diabetic ketoacidosis. Her carbon dioxide of 20 shows it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"In the CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score, which blood urea nitrogen value earns a point?","choices":["Above 10 mg/dL","Above 20 mg/dL","Above 30 mg/dL","Above 40 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Urea above 20 mg/dL (7 mmol/L) earns 1 point; urea means blood urea nitrogen in mg/dL or serum urea in mmol/L. The other points are confusion, respiratory rate of 30 or more, low blood pressure and age 65 or older. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman vomits and aspirates during a witnessed seizure. Two hours later she has abrupt shortness of breath and hypoxemia, a temperature of 100.6°F and a white blood cell count of 13,500/µL. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Supportive care, no antibiotic","Antibiotic with anaerobic coverage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A witnessed aspiration of gastric contents followed within hours by abrupt respiratory decline is aspiration pneumonitis, a sterile chemical injury; fever or leukocytosis can occur without bacterial infection. Support oxygenation and the airway, and avoid prophylactic antibiotics when bacterial infection is not suspected. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 50, 51, 126"},{"q":"The clinical definition of chronic bronchitis requires chronic cough and sputum production for at least how long?","choices":["1 month a year for 1 year","3 months a year for 2 consecutive years","6 months a year for 3 consecutive years","12 months a year for 5 consecutive years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic bronchitis is a clinical definition: chronic cough and sputum production typically for at least 3 months a year for 2 consecutive years. Emphysema, by contrast, is defined anatomically, by destruction and enlargement of the alveoli. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease had 2 moderate exacerbations and 1 hospital admission in the past year. His blood eosinophil count is 350. He is starting maintenance therapy. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["A single bronchodilator","An inhaled corticosteroid alone","Triple inhaled therapy","Daily oral corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two moderate exacerbations and an admission make him high risk, group E. Group E starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic, and adding an inhaled corticosteroid is considered when blood eosinophils are 300 or more, as his 350 are. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 42"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is ready to quit smoking and asks for a medicine to help. He has tried quitting alone twice. Which plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Varenicline with counseling","Varenicline without counseling","Cutting down without a quit date","Tiotropium to ease cravings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every smoking cessation attempt should be combined with counseling, which looks for triggers and gives coping strategies, to increase efficacy. Varenicline, bupropion or nicotine replacement can be added; his failed solo attempts make the combined plan the better choice. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How is severe pulmonary or disseminated histoplasmosis treated?","choices":["Observation without antifungals","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months","Oral itraconazole as an outpatient","Amphotericin-based induction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment follows severity and host risk: severe pulmonary or disseminated histoplasmosis requires specialist-led amphotericin-based induction. Mild acute disease in an immunocompetent adult often needs only observation, and itraconazole is considered for prolonged or progressive illness or higher-risk hosts. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 78, 123"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has resistant hypertension, muscle weakness and a potassium of 2.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.49 is alkalemia, and the bicarbonate of 34 is high and matches it, so it is metabolic. The carbon dioxide of 47 is slight respiratory compensation. Hypertension with hypokalemia points to a hypertensive saline-unresponsive cause such as primary aldosteronism. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with a normal-gap metabolic acidosis has a bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). By the Winter formula, what carbon dioxide range is expected with appropriate compensation?","choices":["24 to 28 mm Hg","25 to 29 mm Hg","33 to 37 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 18 + 8 = 27 + 8 = 35, plus or minus 2, so 33 to 37 mm Hg. A value above 37 would add a respiratory acidosis; below 33, a respiratory alkalosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman being treated for community-acquired pneumonia becomes newly confused. Her heart rate is 118/min, blood pressure 84/50 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 87% on room air, and her urine output has fallen. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Lung abscess","Empyema","Pleurisy","Septicemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When pneumonia becomes systemic it progresses toward bacteremia or septicemia: tachycardia with hypotension, hypoxia, confusion or altered mental status, and decreased urine output or kidney function. She has every one of those signs, which changes her site of care and urgency of support. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 28, 53"},{"q":"A 63-year-old postmenopausal woman with refractory, poorly controlled asthma will need long-term oral corticosteroids to suppress her symptoms. Which addition reduces the bone loss from this therapy?","choices":["Calcium and vitamin D","Cromolyn sodium","Montelukast","Theophylline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When long-term systemic corticosteroids are needed, calcium and vitamin D supplementation should be considered to reduce bone mineral loss, since osteoporosis is a known side effect. Her postmenopausal status adds to the bone risk, and the minimum effective steroid dose is used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. He had anaphylaxis to amoxicillin as a child, and local pneumococcal resistance to macrolides is 35%. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Azithromycin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Doxycycline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A healthy outpatient without resistant-organism risk takes amoxicillin or doxycycline. His amoxicillin anaphylaxis removes amoxicillin, and macrolide monotherapy needs local pneumococcal resistance below 25%, which 35% fails, so doxycycline is the remaining first-line choice. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man who received BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccine in infancy needs testing for tuberculosis infection before starting work in a setting with ongoing transmission. He has no symptoms. Which test is preferred?","choices":["Chest computed tomography","Tuberculin skin test","Acid-fast sputum smear","Interferon-gamma release assay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bacille Calmette-Guérin vaccination can cause a false-positive skin test, and the blood test is preferred for most vaccinated patients because the vaccine does not cause a positive interferon-gamma release assay. It measures immune responses to tuberculosis-specific antigens and needs one visit. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 21, 24"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man who drank methanol is obtunded. Arterial blood gas: pH 6.65 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 18 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 2 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Besides treating the poisoning, what does this pH warrant?","choices":["Therapy to raise the serum pH","No pH-directed therapy","Waiting for respiratory compensation","Paper bag rebreathing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pH below 6.7 is unsustainable: it may cause cardiac hypocontractility and irritability, seizures and cerebral edema, and may warrant therapy to normalize the serum pH along with treatment of the underlying cause. His pH is 6.65. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which diagnostic caution applies to adults 65 and older with suspected chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["The ratio underdiagnoses them","The fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose","Everyone needs alpha-1 testing","Spirometry cannot be used past 65"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In older adults the fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose, so the clinical context is weighed. In adults under 50, lower limits of normal or z-scores help, and in the young or minimally exposed, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is considered. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which neuromuscular disorders are listed as causes of acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Pregnancy and hyperthyroidism","Salicylates and nicotine","Myasthenia gravis and Guillain-Barre syndrome","Muscular dystrophy and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Myasthenia gravis and Guillain-Barre syndrome are the listed neuromuscular causes of acute respiratory acidosis. Muscular dystrophy and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis are the neuromuscular causes of the chronic form. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with heart disease and type 2 diabetes has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. He has no pulmonary hypertension, no cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease), and his hematocrit is 44%. Does he qualify for home oxygen at rest?","choices":["No, 89% needs a listed comorbidity","Yes, in Group I","Yes, in Group II because of diabetes","Yes, because his hematocrit is high"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 89% is above the 88% cutoff, so it needs a listed comorbidity: pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or a hematocrit above 56%. He has none, and his hematocrit is only 44%. Heart disease and diabetes are not on the list, so he does not qualify at rest. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with human immunodeficiency virus infection has a tuberculin skin test read at 48 hours, showing 6 mm of transverse induration. She has no cough, fever or weight loss. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["No action; below the 10 mm cut-off","Repeat the skin test in 8 to 10 weeks","Evaluate for active tuberculosis disease","Start latent-infection treatment now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In human immunodeficiency virus infection the skin test cut-off is 5 mm, so 6 mm is positive. A positive test shows infection, not whether it is latent or active, so clinical evaluation and chest imaging come next to rule out active disease before any preventive therapy. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 22, 41"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has spirometry for suspected asthma. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is reduced with a reduced ratio, and it improves 14% after albuterol. How should this response be read?","choices":["As a positive bronchodilator response","As proof of fixed obstruction","As too small to count","As a reason to skip spirometry next time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Improvement of 12% or more in the forced expiratory volume in 1 second after a bronchodilator is a positive response, and her 14% rise meets it. A positive response on top of reduced baseline values strongly supports the diagnosis of asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 52, 53, 54"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with asthma took aspirin at noon for a headache and developed facial flushing, a runny nose and wheezing at about 10 that night. He asks whether the aspirin could be responsible so many hours later. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["No, aspirin sensitivity causes rash alone","Yes, reactions can be delayed up to 12 hours","Yes, but just selective agents cause it","No, reactions occur within minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In aspirin-sensitive asthma, symptoms may occur within minutes of aspirin ingestion or may be delayed up to 12 hours, and they include rhinorrhea, conjunctival irritation, facial flushing and wheezing. His reaction 10 hours after the dose fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man is confused after drinking antifreeze (ethylene glycol). Sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 102 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is his anion gap?","choices":["30","34","38","46"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate) = 140 - (102 + 8) = 140 - 110 = 30, above the normal 9 to 15: an elevated-gap metabolic acidosis, fitting ethylene glycol, the E of the mnemonic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has had fever, myalgias and cough for 4 days during a week of high influenza activity. She is not hypoxemic and will be treated at home. A rapid influenza antigen test is negative. Which is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Start oseltamivir now","No antiviral after 48 hours","No antiviral; influenza is excluded","Await a molecular test result first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age 65 or older and chronic pulmonary disease make her high-risk, and risk status supports prompt antiviral treatment even after 48 hours. A negative rapid antigen test does not exclude influenza during high activity, and indicated antivirals are not withheld while awaiting confirmation. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 95, 97, 105, 108"},{"q":"Which statement about asthma across the life span is accurate?","choices":["Remission in adolescence is permanent","It worsens in severity with each decade of life","It cannot begin after childhood","It may remit in adolescence and return in adulthood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asthma presents at any age with a peak at 3 years, may become asymptomatic during adolescence, and may return in adulthood. A teenager whose asthma went quiet is not cured and should still be asked about childhood symptoms later in life. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with long-standing heart disease has 3 days of worsening shortness of breath, swelling of both legs, and new wheezing. He has no history of asthma or allergies, and his wheeze began as his heart failure decompensated. Which explanation for his wheezing is most likely?","choices":["Late-onset intrinsic asthma","Cardiac asthma","Extrinsic allergic asthma","Exercise-induced bronchospasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac asthma is wheezing caused by decompensated heart failure. A 74-year-old with heart disease, leg swelling and wheeze that began with the decompensation, and no asthma or atopy, fits it; the heart failure, not the airway, needs treatment. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man hospitalized for 6 days after a stroke develops hospital-acquired pneumonia. He has no methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus risk, no resistance risk and no high mortality risk. Which empiric agent is most appropriate?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Piperacillin-tazobactam","Vancomycin as a single drug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hospital-acquired pneumonia without major resistance or high mortality risk is treated with an antipseudomonal agent that also covers methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus, chosen with the local antibiogram, such as piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime. Respiratory cultures are obtained first. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 39, 42, 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 6 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1. He had 2 moderate exacerbations treated at home in the past year. Under the current three-group scheme, which group is he in?","choices":["Group E","Group A","Group B","Group C"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two moderate exacerbations in the past year make him high risk, and any high-risk patient is group E regardless of symptoms. His low symptom score of 6 does not change that; group E starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has a panic attack before an exam, with light-headedness, perioral numbness and paresthesias (tingling) in both hands. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.53 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 27 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.53 is alkalemia, and the carbon dioxide of 27 is low and matches it, so it is respiratory. Acutely, bicarbonate falls about 2 per 10 mm Hg fall in carbon dioxide: 13 below 40 predicts about 2.6 below the midpoint of 24, roughly 21 to 22, which matches her 22. Psychogenic hyperventilation is a listed cause, and perioral numbness is a listed finding. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 31, 33, 59"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has asthma symptoms 2 days a week, wakes at night with cough twice a month, uses albuterol 2 days a week, and has no limitation of activity. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 88% predicted with a normal ratio, and she had no exacerbations requiring oral steroids this year. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms on 2 or fewer days a week, night awakenings 2 or fewer times a month, rescue use on 2 or fewer days a week, no interference, a 1-second volume above 80% with a normal ratio, and 0 to 1 steroid-requiring exacerbations a year all sit in the intermittent column. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has newly diagnosed drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis. Which regimen is the standard treatment?","choices":["Rifampin alone for 4 months","Four drugs for 2 months, then two for 4","Isoniazid alone for 9 months","Two drugs for 2 months, then four for 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The standard drug-susceptible regimen is isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol for the initial 2 months, then isoniazid and rifampin for 4 months, given daily with directly observed or video-observed therapy to support completion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis has a chest radiograph, a sputum sample sent for a rapid molecular test and a mycobacterial culture, and a tuberculin skin test placed. Which of these results is expected to take weeks?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Mycobacterial culture","Rapid molecular test","Tuberculin skin test reading"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacterium tuberculosis grows slowly, so cultures can take weeks, while molecular tests can provide rapid results. That is why at least one respiratory specimen also gets a rapid molecular test, and why culture-based drug-susceptibility results arrive later. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 20, 26"},{"q":"How many sputum specimens are collected when pulmonary tuberculosis is suspected?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Five"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three sputum specimens are collected 8 to 24 hours apart, with at least one early-morning specimen. Each is sent for an acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, with a rapid molecular test on at least one. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and osteoporosis takes triple inhaled therapy. She has had 2 pneumonias this year and a wrist fracture after a fall. Which component of her regimen is linked to both pneumonia and fracture risk?","choices":["Her as-needed albuterol","The long-acting beta agonist","The long-acting antimuscarinic","The inhaled corticosteroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids increase the risk of pneumonia, and in older adults the concerns include inhaled corticosteroid-related pneumonia and fracture risk. Her repeated pneumonias and fracture make that component the one to reassess. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 42, 53"},{"q":"Which examination finding correlates with the degree of airway obstruction in asthma?","choices":["A shortened expiratory phase","Inspiratory crackles at the bases","A pericardial friction rub","A prolonged expiratory phase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A prolonged expiratory phase during normal respiration correlates with obstruction, because air takes longer to leave through narrowed airways. In severe exacerbations wheezing may disappear, leaving decreased breath sounds with prolonged expiration. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has chronic cough, sputum, fatigue and weight loss. Computed tomography shows fibrocavitary disease, alternative diagnoses have been excluded, and her sputum cultures are negative, but one bronchoalveolar lavage culture grows Mycobacterium avium complex. Which best describes whether she meets the diagnostic criteria?","choices":["Not met; two sputum cultures needed","Not met; a biopsy is required","Met; one positive lavage qualifies","Met on imaging alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nontuberculous lung disease requires all domains: compatible symptoms and imaging (fibrocavitary disease here), exclusion of alternatives, and microbiology. One positive bronchial wash or lavage can meet the microbiologic criterion, even when sputum is negative. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where is a hospitalized patient with possible infectious pulmonary tuberculosis placed?","choices":["Airborne infection isolation room","Contact precautions room","Shared room with masks for both","Standard ward room with a fan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An airborne infection isolation room is used when indicated, staff wear fit-tested N95 or higher respiratory protection, and the patient uses source control during transport. The local or state tuberculosis program is notified. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 33, 13"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a myocardial infarction. Her saturation is 93% on room air. A colleague wants to start oxygen by nasal cannula. Which is the right call?","choices":["Hold oxygen at a saturation of 93%","Start a Venturi mask for her lung disease","Start the nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Start a non-rebreather mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because of vasoconstriction. Her saturation of 93% is above that, so oxygen is held. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9, 15, 16"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with severe persistent asthma also has poorly controlled hypertension, osteoporosis, and a gastric ulcer last year. His asthma remains poorly controlled. Which medication's side effects most threaten his other conditions?","choices":["Short-acting beta-2 agonists","Long-term oral corticosteroids","Leukotriene modifiers","Inhaled corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic corticosteroid side effects include hypertension, osteoporosis and gastric ulceration, all 3 of his other conditions, plus truncal obesity, bruising and depression. If he needs them, the minimum dose that gives control is used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 76, 77"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease says she walks slower than friends her age on level ground because of breathlessness, but she does not need to stop. Which modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade fits?","choices":["Grade 0","Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 2 is walking slower than people of the same age on the level because of breathlessness, or having to stop for breath at one's own pace on the level. A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has been taking large amounts of aspirin; she also has hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic and type 2 diabetes treated with metformin. She is breathing fast. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.52 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 28 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of her medicines best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Aspirin","Her thiazide diuretic","The combination of diuretic and metformin","Metformin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a low carbon dioxide of 28 is respiratory alkalosis, and her bicarbonate of 22 has barely fallen. Salicylates are a listed toxic cause of hyperventilation, and her fast breathing fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who takes long-term prednisone has bronchoscopy to evaluate a lung nodule. A Pneumocystis polymerase chain reaction test on the lavage fluid is positive at a low level. She has no dyspnea, fever or cough, her oxygen saturation is normal on room air, and her imaging shows no infiltrates. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["It must be repeated on expectorated sputum","It calls for adjunctive corticosteroids","It confirms Pneumocystis pneumonia","It may reflect colonization, not infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Pneumocystis polymerase chain reaction is highly sensitive but can detect colonization, so it is interpreted with the symptoms, the imaging and the organism burden. A low-level positive in a woman with no symptoms, a normal oxygen saturation and no infiltrates does not establish pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 62, 65, 67, 72"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with asthma takes ibuprofen for a sprained ankle. Within 20 minutes he develops a runny nose, red irritated eyes, facial flushing and wheezing. Which diagnosis explains the reaction?","choices":["Cardiac asthma","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Aspirin-sensitive asthma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 1 to 5% of asthmatics worsen with aspirin, and patients with true aspirin sensitivity cross-react with non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen. Rhinorrhea, conjunctival irritation, facial flushing and wheezing within minutes of the dose is the described reaction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has been intubated in the intensive care unit for 4 days after a stroke. He develops a new fever, purulent endotracheal secretions and a new infiltrate, and his blood pressure is falling. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Cultures, then empiric antibiotics","Chest computed tomography first","Hold antibiotics until cultures return"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A new infiltrate with fever and purulent secretions more than 48 hours after intubation is ventilator-associated pneumonia. Respiratory specimens are collected, then appropriate empiric antibiotics start without delay, because he is becoming unstable; therapy is later narrowed to the culture results. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 37, 42, 49"},{"q":"Through a nasal cannula, each 1 L/min increase in flow raises the fraction of inspired oxygen by about how much?","choices":["2%","4%","10%","21%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each 1 L/min increase in nasal cannula flow raises the fraction of inspired oxygen by about 4%. For example, 1 L/min gives 24%, 2 L/min gives 28%, and 3 L/min gives 32%, up to 44% at 6 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia remains hypotensive after intravenous fluids and needs a vasopressor infusion to keep up his blood pressure. He is not intubated. How should the severity of his pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Nonsevere: he is not ventilated","Moderate: hospital ward care","Severe: vasopressor-dependent shock","Nonsevere: minor criteria not counted"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe community-acquired pneumonia is vasopressor-dependent shock, mechanical ventilation, or at least three minor criteria. Needing a vasopressor to hold his blood pressure is vasopressor-dependent shock, a major criterion on its own, so he needs urgent intensive care-level assessment and a beta-lactam combination regimen. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 85% on room air on 2 visits, with no cor pulmonale (right heart failure). Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Oxygen just during exercise","No oxygen until saturation falls below 80%","A sleep study for apnea","Long-term oxygen for over 15 hours a day"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe resting desaturation, a saturation of 88% or less at rest, qualifies for long-term oxygen therapy for more than 15 hours a day. Oxygen for severe resting hypoxemia decreases the risk of death, prolongs life and improves quality of life. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 63, 64, 66"},{"q":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman with allergic asthma asks whether keeping her baby away from dust, pets and pollen will prevent the child from developing asthma. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Early exposure guarantees asthma","Avoidance matters just for pet dander","Strict avoidance prevents asthma","Avoidance does not lower the risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled allergens are common triggers and are implicated in sensitization, but avoiding allergens does not reduce the risk of developing asthma, and early exposure may even be protective by building tolerance. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with moderate persistent asthma returns for a scheduled follow-up visit 2 months after starting an inhaled corticosteroid. He reports few symptoms. Which task belongs at this visit?","choices":["Repeat a bronchial provocation test","Stop his written action plan","Review and update his action plan","Start a long-term oral corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Follow-up visits assess and monitor control, review medications and compliance, adjust medications as needed, and review and update the action plan, then schedule the next follow-up. Good control does not remove these tasks. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 43, 90"},{"q":"A 75-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is on a nasal cannula. Her breathing rate and depth change constantly, and her saturation swings with them. Which delivery system would hold her fraction of inspired oxygen steady?","choices":["A partial rebreather mask","A simple oxygen mask","A non-rebreather mask","An air-entrainment system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula's fraction varies with respiratory rate, inspiratory flow and tidal volume, which is why her saturation swings as her breathing changes. An air-entrainment system gives a fixed fraction that does not fluctuate with patient demand, so it would hold her fraction steady. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 8, 14, 15"},{"q":"Which chloride pattern goes with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis?","choices":["Normochloremic","Hyperchloremic","Chloride does not change","Hypochloremic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis is hyperchloremic, since chloride rises as bicarbonate falls. Elevated anion gap acidosis is normochloremic, because other anions replace the bicarbonate. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with a chronic lung disease flare needs both inhaled medication by nebulizer and supplemental oxygen. He has no tracheostomy. Which air-entrainment interface over his face suits him?","choices":["Nasal cannula","Trach collar","Aerosol mask","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He needs a nebulizer for his inhaled medication along with oxygen, and he has no tracheostomy. The aerosol mask is an air-entrainment nebulizer interface that fits over the face, so it delivers oxygen and nebulized medication together. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman with advanced chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, frequent admissions and declining function lives with her daughter, who helps with her care. She has not discussed her wishes for future care. Which step is most appropriate now?","choices":["Wait for the final days of life","Focus on her returning to work","Integrate palliative care early","Add triple therapy and recheck in a year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For older adults, palliative care is integrated early, with caregiver involvement and advance directives, and priorities shift to function, quality of life, multimorbidity and goals of care. Her daughter's involvement makes it timely. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which test is needed to diagnose uncomplicated acute bronchitis?","choices":["A sputum culture","A complete blood count","A chest radiograph","None; it is clinical"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is clinical, made after pneumonia, asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation, influenza, coronavirus disease 2019 and pertussis are considered. Routine blood counts, sputum culture and chest radiography are unnecessary in uncomplicated cases. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a mild exacerbation with increased breathlessness. She is alert, her vital signs are stable, she has no new signs, and her husband is at home with her. Which setting of care is most appropriate?","choices":["Intensive care admission","Outpatient management","Noninvasive ventilation","Hospital ward admission"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. More than 80% of exacerbations can be managed as outpatients. She meets no admission criterion: no marked symptoms despite treatment, no need for noninvasive ventilation, no new signs or arrhythmia, and support at home. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 56, 57"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman hospitalized with coronavirus disease 2019 and hypoxemia is on dexamethasone, but over 24 hours she needs rapidly increasing oxygen. Which treatment should be added?","choices":["Baricitinib or tocilizumab","Add azithromycin","Switch to molnupiravir","Stop dexamethasone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid progression in a hospitalized patient may warrant baricitinib or tocilizumab in addition to corticosteroids, along with appropriate oxygen or ventilatory support. Her rising oxygen needs on dexamethasone are that rapid progression. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 121, 122"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man in septic shock has a lactic acidosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 25 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 11 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How does his acidemia affect oxygen release from hemoglobin to his tissues?","choices":["Release increases; the curve shifts right","Release is unchanged by pH","Release decreases; the curve shifts right","Release decreases; the curve shifts left"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acidosis decreases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, so hemoglobin releases oxygen more readily and delivery to the tissues increases. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman who keeps several parakeets at home is diagnosed with psittacosis and started on doxycycline. One of the birds has been sick. Which additional step is appropriate?","choices":["Reporting is not required","Respiratory isolation at home","Veterinary assessment of the birds","Prophylaxis for household contacts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management includes arranging veterinary assessment of implicated birds and safe cleaning of contaminated areas, reporting according to state requirements and involving public health for outbreaks. Her sick parakeet is the likely source and can infect others. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with no condition that increases his risk of severe respiratory syncytial virus disease asks whether he should get the respiratory syncytial virus vaccine this fall. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["Yes, two doses 4 weeks apart","Yes, one dose every year","Not yet; one dose is due at 75","Yes, one dose now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One respiratory syncytial virus vaccine dose is recommended for all adults 75 or older and for adults 50 to 74 who are at increased risk of severe disease. At 66 with no such risk he is not yet eligible; when he turns 75, one dose is recommended, not a yearly one. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 7, 124"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and diabetes has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. She has had a severe reaction to fluoroquinolone antibiotics as a class. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Moxifloxacin, with QT review","Doxycycline as a single drug","Azithromycin as a single drug","Cefuroxime plus azithromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her lung disease and diabetes are listed comorbidities, calling for amoxicillin-clavulanate, cefpodoxime or cefuroxime plus azithromycin or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone. Her reaction to the fluoroquinolone class takes that option off the table, so the beta-lactam combination is used. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man arrived at a high-altitude camp two hours ago and is hyperventilating; his carbon dioxide is 25 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). Using an acute bicarbonate fall of about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg fall in carbon dioxide, from midpoints of 40 and 24, what bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["16.5 mEq/L","21 mEq/L","24 mEq/L","27 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide fell 15 (40 to 25); acutely bicarbonate falls about 2 per 10, so 24 - 3 = 21 mEq/L. Two hours is acute; altitude is a listed hypoxic cause of hyperventilation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man who smokes has coughed up thick sputum most days for 4 months of each of the last 3 years. He has rhonchi, bluish lips and swelling of both ankles. His blood gas shows low oxygen and raised carbon dioxide, and his diffusing capacity is near normal. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of 0.64. Which subtype best describes his disease?","choices":["Emphysema subtype","Chronic bronchitis subtype","Chronic bronchitis without obstruction","Asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Productive cough for at least 3 months a year over 2 years, rhonchi, cyanosis, edema, early hypoxemia and hypercapnia, and a near-normal diffusing capacity, with confirmed obstruction (ratio 0.64), are the chronic bronchitis picture (the blue bloater). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 14, 15"}]};
